"Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI has announced the public preview of Mistral Large 4, a natively multimodal open-weight model with 1 trillion parameters. Mistral Large 4 (ML4) is a 1-trillion-parameter natively multimodal model with 52 billion active parameters; a preview API is currently available, and weights are scheduled to be released later this month. It delivers state-of-the-art performance among open models for coding, agentic workflows, and multimodal understanding, as well as key enterprise workloads such as cybersecurity, finance, and legal. Trained from scratch on Mistral's own infrastructure of 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs in European datacenters, incorporating training data across more than 160 languages. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral opens a new hub in Munich, Germany—Europe's largest industrial economy—to accelerate European AI sovereignty and collaboration in industrial and physical AI. Mistral establishes a new hub in Munich, Germany, equipped with a research team specialized in industrial and physics AI, along with field engineers. Collaborating on physics-based industrial AI with major German manufacturing enterprises such as BMW (crash simulation) and Siemens Energy. Acquired fluid dynamics and structural mechanics AI capabilities through the acquisition of Emmi AI in May 2026, and announced a target to establish 1 gigawatt (GW) of proprietary computing infrastructure across Europe by 2030. Ensuring AI sovereignty that complies with European regulations without data leaks by running on customers' proprietary infrastructure based on an open-weight architecture. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral AI and Mozilla have partnered to integrate Mistral's open AI models into Mozilla's AI browsing assistant, Firefox Smart Window (beta), enhancing privacy and multilingual support. Mistral models are incorporated into Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window (beta)', supporting tasks such as synthesizing complex searches, recalling previously viewed content, and tab-based information exploration. It is initially available to users in France and North America, with plans to gradually expand support to other regions including the United Kingdom and Germany. It features multilingual optimization tailored to local languages, dialects, and cultural contexts, as well as robust privacy protection based on a zero data retention policy that ensures conversations are not saved on servers by default. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Cloudera and Mistral AI partner to enable enterprise customers, including those in regulated industries, to deploy and train customized AI models while maintaining data sovereignty within their own environments. Mistral AI models are integrated with Cloudera's hybrid data platform, enabling inference execution across on-premises, public/private cloud, and air-gapped environments Supports the creation of custom AI models directly owned and controlled by customers, built upon decades of accumulated proprietary data Addresses the demand for 'Sovereign AI', where customers retain full control over data, intelligence, compute, and operations Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. A case study where Mistral successfully migrated a large, complex Fortran 77 legacy codebase to modern C++ using AI agents and a numerical verification framework. Modernized a 40,000-line Fortran 77 reservoir simulator from a European energy operator into C++, which lacked test code and centralized documentation. Prioritized the establishment of a numerical parity verification framework while refactoring procedural COMMON blocks and global states into an object-oriented structure. Ensured code quality and maintainability prior to migration by combining AI agent-driven code documentation, structured workflows, and human review. Mix, Don't Tune: Bilingual Pre-Training Outperforms Hyperparameter Search in Data-Constrained Settings A research finding demonstrating that when pre-training models for low-resource languages with scarce data, mixing data from high-resource languages is significantly more effective for performance improvement than hyperparameter tuning. A comparative analysis was conducted over approximately 1,000 pre-training runs across 150M to 1.43B parameter scales using a low-resource language (Arabic) and an auxiliary high-resource language (English). Mixing auxiliary language data yielded greater performance gains than hyperparameter tuning in both validation loss and downstream task accuracy, with the gap widening as model size increased. As practical guidance, prioritizing data mix ratio optimization over hyperparameter tuning is recommended, along with transferring hyperparameters from small proxy models via μP. All Systems Go: Global PC App Ecosystem Ready for NVIDIA RTX Spark NVIDIA announced large-scale updates across the global PC software ecosystem in line with the launch of RTX Spark laptops supporting local AI agents as well as creation and development environments. RTX Spark laptops equipped with up to 128GB of unified memory and 1 petaflop of AI compute performance launch on Friday, October 16, accompanied by local AI agent and software optimizations. Major agent apps including GitHub Copilot and Hermes receive updates, the agent-specialized Nemotron 3.5 Bolt 74B model launches on Thursday, October 15, and llama.cpp support is added to NVIDIA PAIR. Key Adobe applications see up to a 2x performance increase, ComfyUI and Blender 5.3 are optimized, and gaming and developer tools such as DLSS 5 are enhanced alongside PyTorch and CUDA support. From Earth to Orbit, France’s Ecosystem Delivers Agentic Computing at Scale With NVIDIA The French industrial ecosystem is adopting and accelerating large-scale agentic computing across diverse sectors including aerospace, finance, public sector, and beauty by leveraging NVIDIA's infrastructure and open models. The expansion of sovereign AI infrastructure is in full swing, highlighted by Bull's production of Vera Rubin Ultra NVL72 servers in Europe and the launch of the Altair-1 satellite equipped with NVIDIA Jetson Orin and Mistral models. Open model-based solutions, including NVIDIA Nemotron, have been deployed across financial platforms (SBS) and sovereign digital workplaces (Twake.ai) serving more than 2,500 French public organizations. Tangible business outcomes are being achieved across various industries, such as accelerating new product research at L'Oreal (up to 100x faster) and reducing seller catalog onboarding time for over 70 million products at Mirakl to under two hours. SpaceXSI bundles X Premium, Grok, and Cursor into one subscription SpaceX Super Intelligence has launched an integrated bundle plan combining X Premium, Grok, and Cursor into one to expand paid subscribers. To secure subscribers in the fiercely competitive paid AI market, it introduced 'The X Subscription', which combines X Premium benefits, the Grok AI chatbot and agents, and the Cursor AI coding service into one. The plans consist of a total of five tiers—Free, Plus ($8/month), Premium ($30/month), Super ($100/month), and Ultra ($200/month)—with higher tiers offering increased AI usage limits. Accessing the latest Grok model requires a Premium plan or higher, and existing users can gradually upgrade to the new bundle via their account billing settings. Just 5 years in, interactive chatbot platform Character.AI files for bankruptcy Customized AI chatbot platform Character.AI has filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection just five years after its founding, amid legal lawsuits and operational difficulties. Character.AI filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection with assets and liabilities between $50 million and $100 million, deciding to pursue a court-supervised sale process. Although it agreed to sell certain assets and a non-exclusive perpetual license of its technology to Disney for $20 million, the service faces the risk of shutting down if a buyer for the entire platform does not emerge. Following early rapid growth and a $2.7 billion licensing deal with Google, the company has faced severe regulatory and legal challenges, including lawsuits related to teen suicide as well as investigations into child safety and privacy. DLSS 5 gets toned down for its Hogwarts Legacy debut next month Nvidia has toned down DLSS 5 to look more natural after facing controversy over excessive generative AI distortions, while improving performance and memory efficiency ahead of its official launch in titles like Hogwarts Legacy next month. DLSS 5, which faced criticism at its initial reveal due to facial distortion and artifacts, has been mitigated to reduce excessive morphing and provide a more natural level of correction. Through the October neural rendering system update, model parameters were reduced by 24% to approximately 118 million, while 4K VRAM usage was cut by 20%. DLSS 5 is scheduled to be rolled out sequentially, starting with NBA 2K27 on October 16th, followed by Hogwarts Legacy on November 5th, and Where Winds Meet late next month. Character.AI files for bankruptcy Generative AI chatbot startup Character.AI has filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection following legal battles and safety controversies, and is pursuing an asset sale to Disney. Character.AI filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection in a U.S. court, with estimated liabilities ranging from $50 million to $100 million. Disney is participating as the lead bidder to acquire key assets and non-exclusive intellectual property (IP) licenses for $20 million. Following its recent hiring of former Character.AI CEO Karandeep Anand as Chief Technology Officer (CTO), Disney is moving toward an 'acquihire' by absorbing the majority of the technical staff. The sale process is scheduled to be completed in late November or early December, and the platform risks being shut down if a suitable bid does not emerge. Z.ai GLM-Legal Beats Claude Fable 5 and Kimi K3 on Harvey LAB Z.ai has launched GLM-Legal, a domain-specific legal AI model based on GLM-5.3-Flash designed for contract review and compliance investigation. GLM-Legal achieved a 19.17% pass rate on Harvey's Legal Agent Benchmark (LAB), ranking 7th out of 79 models. It outperformed Claude Fable 5 (11.25%), Kimi K3 (12.92%), and GPT-6 Astra (5.42%), demonstrating an approximately threefold performance improvement over its base model. It delivers frontier-model-level legal agent capabilities at roughly 1/128 the cost of Claude Fable 5, though it is currently accessible only via an application-based closed beta API. Faithfulness Without Anchors: A Position Paper on the Structural Misalignments of Explainable AI Faithfulness Metrics for Feature Importance This paper presents the structural limitations of faithfulness evaluation metrics for feature importance in Explainable AI (XAI) and proposes research directions to address them. Over the past decade, XAI tools have shown limitations, failing to achieve their original goal of fostering trust and instead inducing over-reliance on erroneous model predictions. Existing XAI techniques oversimplify internal model mechanisms, causing discrepancies (unfaithfulness) between explanations and the model, while current faithfulness metrics struggle to provide reliable quantification or are computationally prohibitive. This paper outlines five major shortcomings of current faithfulness metrics and proposes four future research directions to strengthen reliability. EliteTable: Self-Improving Program Search for Executable Control Policies This study introduces EliteTable, a self-improving program search algorithm that uses an LLM-guided table of high-scoring solutions to evolve executable control policy programs. EliteTable is a self-improving program search algorithm that leverages a fixed-capacity table of high-scoring solutions to propose new candidates, modify existing solutions, and perform recombinations under LLM guidance. Across six Gymnasium control tasks, the median candidate score increased over successive generations, reaching the maximum score ceiling in CartPole-v1 within the very first generation. Task-dependent transfer performance variations were observed: some tasks (BipedalWalker-v3, CarRacing-v3) demonstrated clear performance gains despite falling short of target thresholds, whereas Acrobot-v1 experienced performance degradation during evaluation episodes. Equal-Budget Monte Carlo Tree Search versus Randomized-Restart List Scheduling for DAG Workflows: A Controlled Equal-Budget Computational Study A controlled study demonstrating that Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) underperforms randomized-restart list scheduling when applied to DAG workflow scheduling under identical search budget constraints. In controlled experiments with an evaluation budget of 2,048 schedules, MCTS trailed randomized restart by 2.88% in terms of makespan. While the studied MCTS selection rule deterministically locked onto the first expanded child node and preserved cross-trial correlations, randomized restart was shown to achieve improvements from the tails of the distribution (extreme values). Randomized restart outperformed MCTS on sparse and irregular workflows, showed no significant difference on regular layered workflows, and replacing the mechanism with textbook UCT merely narrowed the deficit without reversing the ranking. Know What You Don't Know: Agentic Data Synthesis by Evolving Generation Space for Closed-Loop Learning This study proposes EvoSyn, a synthetic data generation framework that improves the performance of conversational agents by dynamically expanding and adapting the data generation space based on learner feedback. Existing agentic data synthesis has suffered from a 'feedback-decision gap' where feedback does not directly inform subsequent data generation. EvoSyn enables adaptation and expansion within the generation space by converting feedback into synthesis modification proposals and introducing a role-specialized multi-agent architecture divided between Designer and Planner roles. Experimental results show that it achieves superior performance, improving the average task success rate by 13.3 points over the strongest existing data synthesis baseline. SpireBench: Evaluating Long-Horizon Strategic Play of Agentic Models with Slay the Spire Introduces SpireBench, a novel benchmark evaluating the long-horizon strategic reasoning capabilities of agentic large language models using the commercial deck-building game 'Slay the Spire'. Evaluating 20 LLMs at the highest difficulty revealed that frontier models surpassed heuristic baselines and matched average human performance, yet lagged significantly behind human experts. The performance gap widened as the game progressed, with models demonstrating misjudged risk management, such as over-prioritizing health preservation rather than spending health for long-term growth. Experiments involving renamed in-game terminology confirmed that model performance partially relied on pre-training knowledge, while memory accumulated across multiple playthroughs was error-prone and overly cautious, failing to yield improvements. AutoIndexer: Flexible-Order Decoding by Training Causal Models on Chains of Edits This study proposes AutoIndexer, a generative model based on 'chains of edits' that can flexibly insert, replace, and delete tokens like a text editor to overcome the post-hoc editing limitations of conventional autoregressive models. Points out the limitation that existing autoregressive and diffusion language models struggle to insert or delete tokens at arbitrary positions. Introduces a 'Chain of Edits' approach that flexibly modifies generated text like a text editor by moving the cursor and predicting edit markers. Secures robustness for tasks such as error correction and code revision by perturbing ground-truth labels with reverse-edit operations and training the model to undo them. Achieves post-hoc contextual editing capabilities that existing models lacked, without compromising standard LLM benchmark performance. SWE-Duel: Towards a Self-Scaling Arena for Coding Agents This study proposes 'SWE-Duel', a self-scaling evaluation arena where coding agents generate tasks and evaluate one another through Red-Blue turns to address data contamination and performance saturation issues in conventional static benchmarks. It introduces a mirrored evaluation framework where a Red agent writes new features along with intentional bugs and tests in real-world repositories, and a Blue agent reviews and fixes the pull request without knowing whether a bug exists, enabling mutual verification. A 1,500-turn tournament involving 11 model harnesses was conducted across 15 repositories and 5 programming languages to measure the agents' problem-solving and task-creation capabilities. Tasks generated by newer model versions defeated reference solvers at a significantly higher rate compared to older versions (42.9% vs. 4.0%), confirming that task difficulty self-scales alongside improvements in model capabilities. Contextual Correction of Parallel Drafts for Speculative Decoding Proposes DCOCO, a method that improves the throughput of speculative decoding by addressing the lack of intra-block token dependencies during parallel draft generation through parallel contextual compatibility evaluation. Diffusion model-based draft generators suffer from a lack of intra-block dependencies because their parallel generation nature prevents them from incorporating information from preceding draft tokens. DFlash Contextual Corrector (DCOCO) restores dependencies at minimal extra cost by maintaining multiple candidate tokens instead of a single prediction at each position and evaluating contextual compatibility across adjacent positions in parallel. Evaluations across 4 verification backbones and 10 benchmarks demonstrated up to a 6.6x speedup over vanilla decoding, while achieving 6-23% higher throughput compared to competing methods in serving environments with 4-32 concurrent requests. One Model to Loop Them All: Elastic Latent Recurrence for Language Models This research introduces Elastic Latent Recurrence (ELR), a language model training technique that addresses the performance degradation occurring during single-loop execution, allowing flexible scaling of inference compute and accuracy with a single checkpoint. It mitigates the 'one-loop tax' through three core strategies: gated state updates with learnable step sizes, loop count mixing within optimizer updates, and conditioning based on loop index and budget. By introducing mixed-depth late in pre-training and reusing first-loop expert routing in MoE models, training time was reduced by 11.7%, while maintaining only the last loop's KV cache during inference minimized memory consumption. Experiments with 4B Dense and 30B-A3B MoE hybrid models showed performance improvements of 7.25 points and 4.75 points respectively across 4 iterations, and the resulting Koi-4B model matched Qwen3.5-4B in a single loop while achieving significantly higher scores at 4 loops. Self-Preservation in Language Models This study explores self-preservation in large language models, where models generate replay data and select targets on their own to preserve existing capabilities during continual learning. New tasks and learning rates determine the pattern of degradation in previous capabilities, and tracking previously correct answers captured losses that simple average accuracy might obscure. Replay data generated by a frozen model reduced forgetting on tasks outside the task bank, with the preservation effect further enhanced when combining partial banks. In a 4B parameter model, selecting replay targets by reflecting new task information achieved higher retention rates than random selection, although a trade-off existed with the learning gains on the new task within a fixed budget of examples. Janas - Running LLMs Larger Than RAM by Streaming Experts from SSD Introducing Janas, a local inference engine that enables high-speed execution of MoE language models larger than RAM capacity even on standard PCs by streaming necessary expert weights from NVMe SSDs and caching them in memory. Leverages the characteristics of the MoE architecture to read only the required weights from disk and cache frequently used portions in RAM, running large models on standard PCs without expensive GPUs. Supports speculative decoding, terminal chat, code autocompletion, HTTP API, and MCP integration. An early experimental stage project developed in C, supporting Linux/Windows x86-64. Show GN: Narae Hangul (Naraepack) – HWP/HWPX Editing and Generation CLI, Skills, and Templates Package for AI Agents A current special education teacher has open-sourced 'Narae Hangul', an AI agent-based package of tools for editing and generating HWP and HWPX documents, to automate paperwork in education and public service sectors. It was developed to directly read and edit HWPX (XML) and HWP 5.0 (binary) documents without requiring Hancom Office. Instead of having AI write Python code, it is designed to execute JSON-based task specifications to precisely edit only the necessary cells or tables. It supports four types of operations: inspecting document structure (inspect), editing existing documents (edit), creating new documents (create), and previewing (render), along with providing basic formatting and phrasing templates. Examining the Mathematics of OpenAI's Claimed Faster Fourier Transform Analyzes criticisms of OpenAI's research on a faster Fast Fourier Transform (FFT), which improves theoretical asymptotic complexity but carries an extraordinarily large hidden constant, rendering it an impractical 'galactic algorithm' on real-world computers. Although OpenAI presented a result that theoretically reduces the logarithmic factor slightly compared to conventional FFT, it faces criticism that the hidden constant behind the Big-O notation is excessively large. The algorithm assumes an unrealistically massive complex multiplication as a single operation, requiring an input size that is practically impossible to surpass the traditional Cooley-Tukey FFT. The central issue lies in the disparity with realistic computational models rather than the correctness of the theoretical proof itself, raising concerns over research practices that pursue asymptotic superiority without practical utility. Solar Panels That Plug Directly into Wall Outlets Affordable and simple plug-in solar panels that can be directly connected to wall outlets on apartment balconies and similar spaces are spreading, primarily across the US and Europe. Plug-in solar works by connecting compact panels and an inverter directly to a standard household outlet, allowing even renters to install them without expensive rooftop equipment and offset 10 to 30% of average electricity usage. Following their popularity in Europe—with over 1 million units deployed in Germany—10 states in the US, including Utah and California, have passed legalization bills. While safety concerns such as backfeeding risks and wiring fires have been raised, rapid adoption is expected as testing organizations establish safety standards and certifications. Show GN: claude-bulkhead: A plugin preventing process, port, and memory conflicts across parallel Claude Code sessions on a single PC... Introducing claude-bulkhead, a plugin designed to prevent process, port, and memory conflicts when running multiple Claude Code sessions in parallel on a single PC Inspects processes, ports, and available memory of other sessions right before executing shell commands to proactively block interfering commands Blocks broad process kill commands like pkill and taskkill or git commands targeting other working directories, suggests alternative commands, and supports automatic cleanup of leftover processes Offers an optional bulkhead remote feature to offload heavy workloads to another PC via SSH Machine Learning Tutorial/Workshop [D] Announcement for free machine learning tutorials and workshops hosted on a mathematics Discord server. Free weekly lectures and hands-on sessions covering everything from traditional machine learning to deep learning and graph machine learning The first session begins on Saturday, October 17, and includes hands-on coding and code reviews starting from day one Python basics, NumPy and Matplotlib libraries, and Calculus I knowledge are recommended prerequisites AI peer review tool for CVPR, ICLR, ICML, and NeurIPS papers [D] Introduces 'Paper Review Harness', an open-source tool that simulates pre-submission peer reviews using 30 AI reviewer personas to identify weaknesses in papers before submitting them to major machine learning conferences. Moving beyond simple LLM prompt queries, it provides independent feedback, section-by-section scores, and critiques through 30 diverse reviewer personas. Supports identifying weaknesses across novelty, methodology, experiments, and clarity, prioritized feedback, and a revision workflow that tracks whether previous issues have been addressed. Allows configuring review panels tailored to target conferences such as CVPR, ICLR, ICML, and NeurIPS as well as contribution types, designed as an open-source project that integrates with AI agent environments. Is an MS a soft requirement for Research Engineer / Post-Training roles? [D] A community post asking whether a master's degree is effectively a soft requirement for a data scientist holding a bachelor's degree looking to transition into Research Engineer and Post-Training roles. The author, currently working as a data scientist with a bachelor's degree, is considering a career transition to Research Engineer and Post-Training roles. According to LinkedIn job posting statistics, about half (48% to 56%) of professionals currently in major related roles hold a master's degree. The author seeks advice on how crucial standout credentials—such as conference or workshop papers—or project experiences are for getting hired with only a bachelor's degree. I built a pytorch library of pretrained models for 3D point clouds following timm standards [P] Announcing the development of a PyTorch library that offers a variety of 3D point cloud deep learning models and pretrained weights through a unified interface, following timm design principles. Released the torch-pointcloud library, which provides a consistent interface based on a create_model() factory to resolve complex installation processes and fragmented research codebases. Supports classification, segmentation, detection, and self-supervised learning across various architectures including PointNet++, PointNeXt, and Point Transformer, while hosting pretrained weights via Hugging Face. Simplified CUDA kernel build procedures by reusing prebuilt wheels and ensured benchmark fairness by re-evaluating model performance using a consistent evaluation methodology. How do you handle coverage guarantees from split conformal prediction when the data shifts? [D] This is a question regarding practical approaches to maintaining coverage guarantees in split conformal prediction and handling multi-label predictions in production environments experiencing data drift. Split conformal prediction assumes exchangeability, but in production data, exchangeability often fails due to changes such as newly added categories or phrasing adjustments. The author asks about specific practical remedies, such as periodic recalibration, real-world coverage monitoring and alerts, and employing adaptive conformal prediction variants. The author seeks discussion on decision-making criteria for routing ambiguous cases—where prediction sets contain multiple labels—to humans, LLMs, or fallback rules. Nextflow Frontier posts 4 new top results on BioML-bench Nextflow has unveiled 'Nextflow Frontier', a recursive closed-loop discovery system that combines AI model reasoning with large-scale computational experiments to address unsolved challenges in the life sciences. Nextflow Frontier is an autonomous research system that iteratively performs the entire research lifecycle, from formulating hypotheses and generating executable Nextflow pipelines to running thousands of parallel experiments, analyzing results, and refining solutions. Designed to be problem- and AI model-agnostic, it can explore and apply optimal models and methodologies to any scientific challenge with measurable metrics. It demonstrated remarkable performance, including setting four new top results on the life sciences benchmark BioML-bench. High-speed video reveals how honey bee stingers pump venom This study identifies the mechanism by which honey bee stingers continue to inject venom through a dual-piston pump structure even after the bee has flown away. Honey bees (Apis mellifera) exhibit autotomy, where the stinger continues to bore into tissue and inject venom on its own even after being left behind. The honey bee stinger has an interlocking structure composed of one stylet that forms the main pump chamber and two barbed lancets that slide back and forth. According to research published in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences (PNAS), this unique dual-piston pump mechanism was directly observed and identified for the first time using high-speed videography. Apple’s $299 HomeView smart hub is here: 8 highlights, including the wall model Apple has announced 'HomeView', a $299 smart hub equipped with a touchscreen and generative Siri AI that serves as a central smart home control and security dashboard. Apple announced HomeView, a smart home hub featuring a touchscreen, smart speaker, and security camera dashboard; pre-orders begin on October 13, followed by an official release on November 13. Powered by upgraded Siri AI, it supports natural conversation, multi-command processing, and cross-device context retention, and can recognize up to 6 faces to automatically switch to personalized home screens. It acts as a Matter and HomeKit hub, features smoke and carbon monoxide alarm sound detection alerts, and can display up to 9 camera feeds simultaneously. Dutch tax authorities cancel Microsoft 365 cloud rollout – and turn toward open source The Dutch Tax and Customs Administration has canceled its rollout of the Microsoft 365 public cloud due to data sovereignty and security concerns, pivoting toward on-premises and open-source solutions. The Dutch Tax and Customs Administration has officially withdrawn its plan to migrate to the Microsoft 365 public cloud, which had been scheduled for completion by 2026. An evaluation by the Advisory Council on ICT Assessment cited key reasons for cancellation, including information security, data processing, and loss of data control resulting from the transfer of confidential documents to US servers. Starting with an on-premises deployment for email and calendar services in 2027, the agency plans a phased transition to European open-source collaboration solutions throughout 2027 and 2028. Production Patterns for Agents, Evals, and LLM Costs: QCon AI New York 2026 Introduces QCon AI New York 2026 conference sessions sharing practical patterns from engineers who resolved AI agent authorization management, evaluations (evals), LLM cost reduction, and code review bottlenecks in real-world production environments. Engineers from AWS and LinkedIn share their experiences building agent authorization models and guardrails operating at a scale of over 500,000 nodes. Covers DoorDash's evaluation loop for agentic ordering systems and approaches by a former Netflix engineer to reduce LLM token costs through prompt compression and caching. Production operational know-how is presented on pre-filtering low-confidence suggestions to reduce developer bottlenecks, exemplified by LinkedIn's AI code review system. \"Surpassed Opus 5.5\"... Anthropic Testing Real-World Routing for Next-Generation Model Circumstances indicate that Anthropic is conducting real-world routing and shadow deployment tests of its next-generation AI model, which surpasses the existing Opus 5.5, leveraging its flagship environment. Just three weeks after releasing Opus 5.5, Anthropic is conducting live routing tests that divert a portion of traffic to an unreleased next-generation model. Testers confirmed performance that overwhelms the existing model in tasks such as Blender MCP integration, JavaScript-based animation creation, and multimodal tool integration. An Anthropic PR manager heightened expectations for the upcoming model by leaving a comment responding to user reactions stating, 'The best is yet to come.' Controversy Over Plagiarism in Decision-Making Model 'Zev'...'Copied Research from a Year Ago' Allegations have emerged that TypeSafe's decision-making model 'Zev' plagiarized prior research from a year ago, intensifying competition in decision-making model technology. Nandakishore Mukkunoth of ConvAI Innovations claimed that TypeSafe's decision-making model 'Zev' plagiarized the architecture of his research conducted a year ago. Zev is a high-speed, cost-effective model specialized for decision-making that directly calculates option probabilities instead of generating text, utilizing them in conditional statements and other workflows. Amid the controversy, Mukkunoth released the open-source model 'Raya', accelerating competition in developing similar decision-making models across big tech and the open-source community. OpenAI Significantly Expands Context Window for 'GPT-6.1 Sol' to 500k Tokens OpenAI has increased the default context window for 'GPT-6.1 Sol', utilized in 'Codex' and 'ChatGPT Work', to 500,000 tokens, representing an approximate 80% expansion. The default context window has significantly increased from the previous 272,000 tokens to 500,000 tokens, reducing context compression and loss of details during extended workflows. Announced as the Day 7 update of OpenAI's 28-day continuous Codex performance improvement event, with plans for further expansion also revealed. Users can manually adjust context settings as needed, with GPT-6.1 Sol supporting a maximum context window of up to 1 million tokens. Microsoft Unveils 'Code Video Model' for Precise Control of In-Video Physics via Code Microsoft has unveiled a 'Code Video Model' that utilizes executable code to precisely control object movements, laws of physics, and camera trajectories within videos. A coding agent writes code applying physical equations based on prompts to generate a proxy video within one minute, which is then combined with an R2V model to produce the final video. Through an 'early-stage conditioning' technique that references the proxy video only during the initial sampling phase, it achieves both structural control and natural visual quality simultaneously. In self-benchmark evaluations, it significantly outperformed the existing VACE model, with expected expansions into robotics simulation and world model data generation in the future. Decision-Making Models Evolve into Multimodal... vLLM and Cloudflare Release Open-Source Models vLLM and Cloudflare have released open-source multimodal decision-making models that directly process diverse inputs, such as images and videos, to make determinations based on choice probabilities. The vLLM development team released the 'Decision 3.0' family (0.8B to 27B) under the Apache 2.0 license, featuring a built-in vision encoder that directly analyzes images without separate preprocessing. Instead of generating lengthy text, decision-making models return probabilities for each choice, simplifying conditional branching and routing pipelines for agents while reducing latency and costs. Cloudflare also introduced 'Clef-Omni' as open weights, a 30B MoE-based multimodal decision-making model that simultaneously analyzes text, images, audio, and video. HCLTech Delivers Strong Q2 Results Driven by Record $3.3 Billion Deal Wins Global IT firm HCLTech delivered strong Q2 FY2027 financial results and announced a partnership with Anthropic, powered by record new deal wins and surging advanced AI revenue. HCLTech recorded new deal wins of $3.3 billion (up 27% year-over-year), reaching its highest-ever first-half bookings and posting Q2 revenue of $3.7 billion. Advanced AI revenue surged 78% year-over-year, reaching an annualized run rate of $876 million. The company entered into a global premier partnership with Anthropic to accelerate enterprise adoption of agentic AI and plans to build Claude-powered industry solutions. Ambi, an AI Assistant Founded by Plaud Alums, Raises $26M (approx. 34.7 Billion KRW) Ambi, an AI assistant startup founded by alumni of AI device company Plaud, has raised approximately 34.7 billion KRW in funding and is expanding its hardware and software development. AI startup Ambi has raised a total of $26 million (approx. 34.7 billion KRW) in an investment round led by Luminous Ventures. Ambi offers an AI assistant app for Apple devices and is developing an 'Ambient Capture' feature that records ambient conversations, a desktop app that observes screens, and dedicated hardware devices. While audio recordings are not stored and are encrypted, recording notification and privacy issues remain industry-wide challenges. Kakao AI Learns Social Cues to Initiate Chat: Dialogue Training in Virtual KakaoTalk Kakao is advancing its AI proactive chat feature to naturally initiate conversations based on dialogue context by leveraging 'Clone' virtual chat simulation technology and an on-device/server hybrid architecture. Kanana in KakaoTalk employs a hybrid architecture where an ultra-lightweight 1.3B on-device model summarizes conversation context and decides whether to intervene, collaborating with a server model to generate proactive messages. To resolve context errors and out-of-the-blue messages, Kakao established 'Clone' simulation technology, enabling AI agents to train by conversing with each other in a virtual KakaoTalk environment. Adopting the Clone-based pipeline increased expressive diversity by 13% and shortened the model deployment cycle from one or two months to approximately two weeks, with future plans to evolve into proactive AI that autonomously learns the appropriate level of intervention. HCLTech and Anthropic Form Global Premier Partnership to Drive Enterprise Governance-Based Agentic AI Adoption Worldwide HCLTech and Anthropic have entered into a global premier partnership to accelerate the adoption of agentic AI equipped with safety and governance. HCLTech will establish a dedicated Anthropic business unit and CoE to develop industry-specific solutions powered by Claude, Cowork, and Claude Code. As an ISV partner of Anthropic, HCLSoftware will embed Claude capabilities into enterprise software products such as HCL BigFix and HCL AppScan. By integrating the Claude product family into its proprietary engineering platform AI Force, it will deliver IT support and code remediation workflows grounded in policy guardrails and human oversight. Kolon Benit Unveils 6-Stage AX Execution Strategy to Drive Business Results Beyond AI Adoption Kolon Benit has unveiled a detailed 6-stage AX execution strategy and on-site implementation cases aimed at moving beyond AI adoption to achieve tangible business outcomes and workflow innovation. Kolon Benit hosted the 'AX Boost Summit 2026' and presented a 6-stage AX methodology focused on workflow redesign and performance measurement rather than simple technology adoption. While 58% of domestic enterprises utilize AI, only 27% possess a concrete strategy, with scattered data and insufficient integration with business systems cited as key reasons for the lack of tangible results. Kolon Industries shared specific success cases, including reducing processing time from 4 hours to 5 seconds per case with a shipping documentation automation agent and achieving a 99.8% accuracy rate with Vision AI identification."